    "knowledge_point": "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions",
    "problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{ln⁡(x)},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e.$",
    "worked_solution": "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=ln⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot ln⁡x-{x}^{2}\\cdot (ln⁡x)^{′}}{(ln⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cdot ln⁡(x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})}{{(ln⁡(x))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cdot ln⁡(x)-x}{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(2ln⁡(x)-1)}{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{e(2ln⁡(e)-1)}{{ln}^{2}⁡(e)} \\\\  & =\\frac{e(2-1)}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e.$",
